<section>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">Section 6.2 Exercises</heading>
    <exercise number="58" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Derive the formula for the volume of a sphere using the slicing method.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="59" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the slicing method to derive the formula for the volume of a cone.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="60" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the slicing method to derive the formula for the volume of a tetrahedron with side length $$a.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="61" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the disk method to derive the formula for the volume of a trapezoidal cylinder.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="62" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Explain when you would use the disk method versus the washer method. When are they interchangeable?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, draw a typical slice and find the volume using the slicing method for the given volume.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="63" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A pyramid with height 6 units and square base of side 2 units, as pictured here.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>8 units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="64" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A pyramid with height 4 units and a rectangular base with length 2 units and width 3 units, as pictured here.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="65" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A tetrahedron with a base side of 4 units, as seen here.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$32323232$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="66" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A pyramid with height 5 units, and an isosceles triangular base with lengths of 6 units and 8 units, as seen here.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="67" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A cone of radius $$r$$ and height $$h$$ has a smaller cone of radius $$r/2$$ and height $$h/2$$ removed from the top, as seen here. The resulting solid is called a frustum.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$724\pi r2h724\pi r2h$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, draw an outline of the solid and find the volume using the slicing method.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="68" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The base is a circle of radius $$a.$$ The slices perpendicular to the base are squares.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="69" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The base is a triangle with vertices $$\left( {0,0} \right),\left( {1,0} \right),$$ and $$\left( {0,1} \right).$$ Slices perpendicular to the x-axis are semicircles.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\pi 24\pi 24$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="70" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The base is the region under the parabola $$y = 1 - x^{2}$$ in the first quadrant. Slices perpendicular to the xy-plane and parallel to the y-axis are squares.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="71" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The base is the region under the parabola $$y = 1 - x^{2}$$ and above the $$x \text{-axis} .$$ Slices perpendicular to the $$y \text{-axis}$$ are squares.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$22$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="72" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The base is the region enclosed by $$y = x^{2}$$ and $$y = 9.$$ Slices perpendicular to the x-axis are right isosceles triangles. The intersection of one of these slices and the base is the leg of the triangle.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="73" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The base is the area between $$y = x$$ and $$y = x^{2}.$$ Slices perpendicular to the x-axis are semicircles.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\pi 240\pi 240$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, draw the region bounded by the curves. Then, use the disk method to find the volume when the region is rotated around the x-axis.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="74" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x + y = 8,x = 0, \text{and} y = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="75" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = 2x^{2},x = 0,x = 4, \text{and} y = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$4096\pi 54096\pi 5$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="76" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = e^{x} + 1,x = 0,x = 1, \text{and} y = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="77" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = x^{4},x = 0, \text{and} y = 1 \text{for} x \geq 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$8\pi 98\pi 9$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="78" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \sqrt{x},x = 0,x = 4, \text{and} y = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="79" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \sin x,y = \cos x, \text{and} x = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\pi 2\pi 2$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="80" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \frac{1}{x},x = 2, \text{and} y = 3$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="81" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x^{2} - y^{2} = 9 \text{and} x + y = 9,y = 0 \text{and} x = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\int 4 e 3 x d x = - 4 3 e -3 x + C \int 4 e 3 x d x = - 4 3 e -3 x + C$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, draw the region bounded by the curves. Then, find the volume when the region is rotated around the y-axis.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="82" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = 4 - \frac{1}{2}x,x = 0, \text{and} y = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="83" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = 2x^{3},x = 0,x = 1, \text{and} y = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$4\pi 54\pi 5$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="84" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = 3x^{2},x = 0, \text{and} y = 3$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
